e wall. If I get another B7, I can form a pung. Since B7 is a middle tile, forming a pung could add points. Also, having three B7s in hand is a lot. If I play one, it reduces the number, but keeps the possibility of a pung. Alternatively, keeping B7s might help in forming a pair or pung.\n\n- Play T4: I have T4 in my hand. The meld [T1, T2, T3] is there. If I have T4, maybe I can form a chow with T3-T4-T5 if I get a T5. But T5 is still available (unshown count is 4). So if I play T4, maybe someone can chi it if they have T3 or T5. The player across has a meld of W6-W7-W8, so not relevant. The next player has a meld of B3-B4-B5, so maybe not. Playing T4 might be a safe tile.\n\n- Play W6: I have W6 in my hand. The meld [W1, W2, W3] is there. W6 could be part of a chow with W5-W6-W7, but I don't have W5. The player across has a meld of W6-W7-W8, so they have W6. If I play W6, maybe they can chi it to complete their W6-W7-W8. But since they already have a W6 in their meld, maybe they don't need it. Alternatively, if I play W6, maybe someone else can chi it. Not sure.\n\nConsidering the current hand, I have two chows, and the rest are individual tiles. To improve my hand, I need to form more melds. The B7s could be a pung if I get another one. Since there are three left, it's possible. Playing one B7 now might be a good move to reduce the hand and set up for a pung. Also, having three B7s in hand is a lot, and playing one reduces the risk of being blocked. If I can form a pung of B7, that adds points. Additionally, the B7s are suited tiles, so a pung would contribute to scoring patterns like All Pungs or All Simples.\n\nAnother consideration is the current seat wind and prevailing wind. My seat wind is East, and the prevailing is South. I have no pungs of winds or dragons, so Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind points might not apply here. But if I can form a pung of B7, that's a Pure Pung, which might contribute to All Simples or other patterns.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles